	(text "Aiming for 600-680 total capacitance\nfor around 50-60Hz cut-off.\n\nNote that it does not mean low frequencies\nwill be perceived significantly lower.\nThe damping factor below the cut-off is\nsignificantly reduced, which would create\nmore low end “bloom”, and more perceivable\ndeepness.\n\nMore caps are better to distribute current\n(make sure ESR is consistent so some caps\nare not overworking over others).\nThis way you would not need big current\nripple rating for the caps.\nSo you can use smaller values but put\na big grid of them, so the current is\ndistributed between all of them."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 162.306 65.532 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "02dded4d-757c-43cb-b89a-1543ec3260e8")
	)
	(text "The power amp IC are all TDA7294"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 149.098 131.826 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 3.81 3.81)
				(thickness 0.762)
				(bold yes)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "871eb2e0-2d57-4838-9907-d24e91fb02ed")
	)
	(text "40W-50W of power for each amplifier channel\nat ±24V supply into 4 ohms (as “seen” by the\namplifier, you can connect 8 or 16 ohms cab/speaker\ninto the respective transformer secondary port,\nso that for the amplifier it stays 4 ohms load).\nIn my experience it’s enough to power\n2x12/4x12 cab with just one channel at ±24V supply.\n\nEverything is designed around 4 ohms\nload. The impedance transformers are\nsupposed to keep that 4 ohms load\nconsistency regardless of actual\nspeaker load you connect it to.\n\nMaximum supply is ±29V\n(for 100W output per-channel, current limited)\nfor 4 ohms load. I would say ±27V is a safe\nsweet spot for plenty of power.\n\nYou can technically power it with ±36V to ±38V/±40V (100W)\nfor 8 ohms load but several parts would have to be redesigned\nfor 8 ohms load to preserve same output cut-offs,\ndamping factor calculations, etc.\n\nI successfully tested the amp at proper volumes\nwith 2 affordable 90W 24V power bricks (one for V+\nand one for V-). It’s easy to find relatively\ncheap 24V switching-mode bricks from affordable\nHiFi Class D amps manufacturers that are pretty quiet\nand powerful enough, designed to feed audio amplifiers.\nYou just need to make sure that the power supply\ndoes not reference neither positive nor negative end\nto ground."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 345.44 203.454 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "8cd8145d-88fd-47c7-91eb-0874c2cc61fe")
	)
